// Mehlman HY Arrows - physiologic up/down relationships
const arrowsQuestions = [
  // ===== ARROWS: RAAS and Adrenal =====
  { id: "ar1", category: "RAAS", q: "High aldosterone - arrows for serum Na+, K+, pH, bicarb, CO2?", a: "↑Na+, ↓K+, ↑pH, ↑bicarb, ↑CO2. All arrows go the same direction as aldosterone EXCEPT potassium. Aldosterone upregulates the basolateral Na/K-ATPase in the cortical collecting duct -> Na reabsorption and K secretion, water follows Na. It also upregulates an apical H+-ATPase -> protons into the urine -> metabolic alkalosis. CO2 is acidic, so respiratory rate slows to retain it as compensation." },
  { id: "ar2", category: "RAAS", q: "Low aldosterone - arrows for serum Na+, K+, pH, bicarb, CO2?", a: "↓Na+, ↑K+, ↓pH, ↓bicarb, ↓CO2. Same rule: everything follows aldosterone except potassium. This is a normal-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation. Per Mehlman, knowing high vs low aldosterone arrows is paramount for Step 1." },
  { id: "ar3", category: "RAAS", q: "Lisinopril - arrows for renin, angiotensin I, angiotensin II, aldosterone?", a: "↑renin, ↑AT I, ↓AT II, ↓aldosterone. ACE inhibitor blocks AT I -> AT II in the lungs. Lower aldosterone -> lower fluid status -> renin rises to compensate -> more angiotensinogen cleaved to AT I. Rule: block a step and substrates on the left go up, products on the right go down." },
  { id: "ar4", category: "RAAS", q: "Valsartan - arrows for renin, angiotensin I, angiotensin II, aldosterone?", a: "↑renin, ↑AT I, ↑AT II, ↓aldosterone. An ARB blocks the receptor but does not block AT II synthesis, so AT II rises while aldosterone synthase activity in the zona glomerulosa falls. Lower aldosterone -> lower volume -> renin up -> more AT I -> more AT II." },
  { id: "ar5", category: "RAAS", q: "Spironolactone - arrows for renin, AT I, AT II, aldosterone, K+?", a: "All ↑ - renin, AT I, AT II, aldosterone, and K+. Blocking the aldosterone receptor backs up every substrate. Since aldosterone normally secretes K into the urine, blocking it causes hyperkalemia; ACE inhibitors and ARBs also raise K by lowering aldosterone. Spironolactone is anti-androgenic and causes gynecomastia; eplerenone has less of that risk." },
  { id: "ar6", category: "RAAS", q: "Enalapril - arrows for LV afterload, systemic arteriolar diameter, renal afferent diameter, renal efferent diameter?", a: "↓LV afterload, ↑systemic arteriolar diameter, ↔afferent diameter, ↑efferent diameter. AT II is a potent vasoconstrictor of peripheral arterioles and of the EFFERENT arteriole (which raises filtration fraction and maintains GFR when renal blood flow drops). Per Mehlman, do not overthink minor afferent effects - the exam treats AT II as an efferent constrictor, so ACEi/ARBs increase efferent diameter." },
  { id: "ar7", category: "RAAS", q: "32F with an abdominal bruit and BP 160/100 - arrows for Na+, K+, pH, bicarb, CO2?", a: "↑Na+, ↓K+, ↑pH, ↑bicarb, ↑CO2. Fibromuscular dysplasia (tunica media proliferation, women 20s-50s) -> decreased renal perfusion -> renin from juxtaglomerular cells -> RAAS activation. Renal artery stenosis gives the same arrows but the vignette is atherosclerotic: claudication, prior CABG, years of smoking." },
  { id: "ar8", category: "Adrenal", q: "74M with forearm hyperpigmentation, 23% eosinophils, 6 months of fatigue, BP 100/60 - arrows for Na+, K+, pH, bicarb, CO2, ACTH?", a: "↓Na+, ↑K+, ↓pH, ↓bicarb, ↓CO2, ↑ACTH. Addison disease (primary hypoadrenalism), usually autoantibodies against 21-hydroxylase - both aldosterone and cortisol are low. Low cortisol means less negative feedback -> ↑CRH and ACTH; POMC is the precursor to ACTH and alpha-MSH, which causes the hyperpigmentation. Eosinophilia is common because cortisol normally sequesters eosinophils in the spleen - do not go chasing stool ova and parasites." },
  { id: "ar9", category: "Adrenal", q: "Primary vs secondary hypoadrenalism - which hormones are low?", a: "Addison (primary): the adrenal gland itself is damaged, so BOTH aldosterone and cortisol are low. Secondary (low pituitary ACTH): only cortisol is low, because the gland is fine and AT II can still drive aldosterone synthesis." },
  { id: "ar10", category: "Adrenal", q: "45M with fatigue and darkening forearms, BP 100/60, and only a marginal cortisol rise after exogenous ACTH - arrows before the test for ACTH, eosinophils, Na+, K+, pH, bicarb, CO2?", a: "↑ACTH, ↑eosinophils, ↓Na+, ↑K+, ↓pH, ↓bicarb, ↓CO2. Addison. A healthy patient shows a robust cortisol rise to exogenous ACTH; a marginal rise proves adrenal insufficiency. Low aldosterone means less Na reabsorption and less K and proton secretion in the cortical collecting duct, so retained protons drop the bicarb, and CO2 is blown off to compensate." },
  { id: "ar11", category: "Adrenal", q: "39F on long-term prednisone with a Cushingoid appearance - arrows for ACTH and cortisol?", a: "↓ACTH and ↓cortisol. Students get this wrong constantly: prednisone is a glucocorticoid ANALOGUE, not cortisol itself. It suppresses CRH and ACTH, so measured serum cortisol falls even though the patient looks Cushingoid." },
  { id: "ar12", category: "Adrenal", q: "13M treated for meningococcal septicemia three days ago now has BP 80/50 - arrows for aldosterone, cortisol, ACTH, K+, Na+, HCO3-, pH, CO2?", a: "↓aldosterone, ↓cortisol, ↑ACTH, ↑K+, ↓Na+, ↓HCO3-, ↓pH, ↓CO2. Waterhouse-Friderichsen: hemorrhagic necrosis of the adrenals from meningococcal septicemia, hitting both zona glomerulosa and zona fasciculata. Cortisol normally upregulates alpha-1 receptors on arterioles, so without it norepinephrine and epinephrine cannot maintain BP." },
  { id: "ar13", category: "Adrenal", q: "After normal saline, which drug do you give in Waterhouse-Friderichsen, and why not norepinephrine?", a: "Hydrocortisone, a cortisol analogue. Norepinephrine will not raise BP much because alpha-1 receptors are not adequately expressed without cortisol." },
  { id: "ar14", category: "Cushing", q: "31M Cushingoid with undetectable ACTH and high cortisol - what happens to ACTH and cortisol with high-dose dexamethasone?", a: "↔ACTH, ↔cortisol. Low/undetectable ACTH with high cortisol means Cushing syndrome from primary adrenal cortical secretion - adenoma or diffuse cortical hyperplasia. Dexamethasone would only suppress ACTH further, and it is already undetectable, so nothing moves (per the NBME)." },
  { id: "ar15", category: "Cushing", q: "Cushing syndrome vs Cushing disease?", a: "Cushing syndrome is how the patient looks - any cause: exogenous glucocorticoids, an ACTH-secreting pituitary tumor, a cortisol-secreting adrenal tumor, or small cell bronchogenic carcinoma secreting ACTH. Cushing DISEASE specifically means an ACTH-secreting tumor of the anterior pituitary." },
  { id: "ar16", category: "Cushing", q: "How do you read the low-dose and high-dose dexamethasone suppression tests?", a: "Low-dose dex is a yes/no test for Cushing syndrome only: cortisol suppresses = no Cushing syndrome; does not suppress = Cushing syndrome of unknown cause. Then high-dose dex: cortisol suppresses = Cushing DISEASE (only pituitary tumors suppress with high dose). Does not suppress = primary adrenal (adenoma/hyperplasia) if ACTH is LOW, or small cell bronchogenic carcinoma if ACTH is HIGH. If the patient is on exogenous glucocorticoids you do not run the test at all - the cause is obvious." },
  { id: "ar17", category: "Cushing", q: "45F Cushingoid with high ACTH, high cortisol, and a pituitary mass on MRI - what does high-dose dexamethasone do?", a: "↓ACTH, ↓cortisol. Cushing disease suppresses with high-dose dex; ACTH from a small cell bronchogenic carcinoma would not." },
  { id: "ar18", category: "Cushing", q: "50M Cushingoid with proximal weakness who CAN hold upward gaze 60 seconds, high ACTH and cortisol - what does high-dose dexamethasone do?", a: "↔ACTH, ↔cortisol. He has Lambert-Eaton plus Cushing syndrome from small cell bronchogenic carcinoma, so it is not Cushing disease and will not suppress. Lambert-Eaton patients CAN maintain upward gaze for 60 seconds; myasthenia gravis patients cannot - that detail shows up a lot on NBMEs." },
  { id: "ar19", category: "Cushing", q: "35F with central obesity, purple striae, and an adrenal mass on CT - arrows for ACTH, cortisol, urinary potassium?", a: "↓serum ACTH, ↑cortisol, ↑urinary potassium. Cortisol-secreting adenoma of the zona fasciculata; ACTH is suppressed by negative feedback. Chronically high glucocorticoid wastes potassium distally like aldosterone, so a K+ of 3.0 in a Cushingoid patient is expected - do not conclude aldosterone is high." },
  { id: "ar20", category: "Pituitary", q: "28F one week after a C-section with heavy blood loss, now fatigued and unable to breastfeed - arrows for prolactin, ACTH, TSH, aldosterone?", a: "↓prolactin, ↓ACTH, ↓TSH, ↑aldosterone. Sheehan syndrome: the pituitary doubles in size in pregnancy to make prolactin, raising oxygen demand, so heavy blood loss causes ischemic infarction. Low TSH here means SECONDARY hypothyroidism. Per Mehlman the aldosterone arrow is odd but is straight off a retired NBME - low ACTH means low cortisol, less alpha-1 upregulation, lower BP, and that drives aldosterone up relative to baseline." },
  { id: "ar21", category: "Cushing", q: "40M with weight gain, a pituitary mass, L1 point tenderness with a compression fracture, and slight weakness - arrows for prolactin, ACTH, K+?", a: "↔prolactin, ↑ACTH, ↓K+. Point tenderness over a vertebra means compression fracture means osteoporosis; Cushing disease can present as just osteoporosis and weight gain (do not confuse with avascular necrosis of the femoral head, also seen in Cushing). Prolactin is unchanged because this is not a prolactinoma." },
  { id: "ar22", category: "Steroids", q: "Which glucocorticoid has strong mineralocorticoid effect?", a: "Fludrocortisone, often used in Addison. Endogenous cortisol, prednisone, hydrocortisone, and dexamethasone have only minor mineralocorticoid effect - but chronically high levels still lower serum potassium." },
  { id: "ar23", category: "ADH", q: "ADH is HIGH - arrows for serum sodium, urinary osmolality, urinary specific gravity?", a: "↓serum sodium, ↑urinary osmolality, ↑urinary specific gravity. ADH inserts aquaporins in the medullary collecting duct -> more free water reabsorbed -> serum sodium diluted, urine concentrated. High ADH happens in dehydration and SIADH." },
  { id: "ar24", category: "ADH", q: "ADH is LOW - arrows for serum sodium, urinary osmolality, urinary specific gravity?", a: "↑serum sodium, ↓urinary osmolality, ↓urinary specific gravity. Fewer aquaporins in the medullary collecting duct -> less free water reabsorbed -> serum is more concentrated and the urine is dilute. Central DI is decreased ADH production by the hypothalamus (or defective posterior pituitary storage); in nephrogenic DI ADH is HIGH because the kidney is insensitive to it." },
  { id: "ar25", category: "ADH", q: "45M smoker with weight loss and confusion - arrows for serum sodium, urinary osmolality, urinary specific gravity?", a: "↓serum sodium, ↑urinary osmolality, ↑urinary specific gravity. SIADH from small cell bronchogenic carcinoma. Mental status change happens with either low or high sodium." },
  { id: "ar26", category: "Paraneoplastic", q: "Paraneoplastic syndromes of small cell vs squamous cell lung cancer?", a: "Small cell: SIADH (ADH), Cushing syndrome (ACTH), Lambert-Eaton (pre-synaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channel antibodies), and cerebellar/neurologic dysfunction (anti-Hu, anti-Yo). Squamous cell: hypercalcemia from PTH-related peptide, often presenting as urolithiasis." },
  { id: "ar27", category: "ADH", q: "17M hearing voices telling him to drink to cleanse himself - arrows for serum sodium, urinary osmolality, urinary specific gravity?", a: "↓serum sodium, ↓urinary osmolality, ↓urinary specific gravity. Psychogenic polydipsia from schizophreniform disorder (psychosis 1-6 months). ADH is APPROPRIATELY low so free water is peed out (dilute urine), but serum sodium is still low from the water load. The bodybuilder drinking gallon jugs is the same question." },
  { id: "ar28", category: "ADH", q: "30M with head trauma making 800 mL/hour of urine - arrows for serum sodium, urinary osmolality, urinary specific gravity?", a: "↑serum sodium, ↓urinary osmolality, ↓urinary specific gravity. Central DI from head trauma. Head trauma and meningitis can cause EITHER central DI or SIADH - the huge dilute urine output tells you it is DI." },
  { id: "ar29", category: "Tonicity", q: "24F on lithium, tremulous, with increased urine output - tonicity of the PCT, JGA, and MCD relative to serum?", a: "↔(isotonic) PCT, ↓(hypotonic) JGA, ↓(hypotonic) MCD. Nephrogenic DI from lithium. The rules: the PCT is ALWAYS isotonic, the JGA is ALWAYS hypotonic (it sits at the early DCT above the thick ascending limb, where massive Na/K/2Cl reabsorption dilutes the urine), and the MCD is hypotonic in central DI, nephrogenic DI, and psychogenic polydipsia, but hypertonic in SIADH and dehydration." },
  { id: "ar30", category: "Tonicity", q: "32F marathon runner 10 miles in with nothing to drink - tonicity of PCT, JGA, MCD relative to serum?", a: "↔PCT (always isotonic), ↓JGA (always hypotonic), ↑MCD (hypertonic in dehydration and SIADH). Very important Step 1 question." },
  { id: "ar31", category: "ADH", q: "47F on large doses of ibuprofen for 25 years - arrows for urine volume and urine osmolality in response to exogenous ADH?", a: "↔urine volume, ↔urine osmolality. Analgesic nephropathy from long-term NSAIDs causes nephrogenic DI. The kidney is insensitive to ADH and endogenous ADH is already maximally high, so giving more does nothing. Normally ADH would raise urine osmolality and lower urine volume via aquaporins in the MCD." },
  { id: "ar32", category: "ADH", q: "24M given IV mannitol - immediate arrows for ADH and serum osmolality?", a: "↑ADH, ↑serum osmolality. Mannitol is a sugar alcohol, so infusing it adds solute to the serum immediately; higher osmolality triggers ADH release to reabsorb free water and re-dilute the serum. Rule: higher serum tonicity -> more ADH; lower serum tonicity -> less ADH." },
  { id: "ar33", category: "Pharm", q: "How does mannitol work, and who should not get it?", a: "Osmotic diuretic exerting oncotic pressure in the PCT and thin descending limb, holding water in the tubule to cause diuresis. It is the 2CK answer for lowering intracranial pressure in a patient already being hyperventilated (low CO2 reduces cerebral perfusion). Avoid it in heart failure - the initial rise in plasma volume transiently increases preload." },
  { id: "ar34", category: "Thyroid", q: "Primary vs secondary HYPERthyroidism - arrows for TSH, T3, T4?", a: "Primary: ↓TSH, ↑T3, ↑T4 (Graves, toxic adenoma, toxic multinodular goiter, and transient thyroiditis). Secondary: ↑TSH, ↑T3, ↑T4 (a TRH- or TSH-secreting tumor). A retired NBME asks this point-blank with a two-line stem: TSH and T4 both elevated = secondary hyperthyroidism." },
  { id: "ar35", category: "Thyroid", q: "Primary vs secondary HYPOthyroidism - arrows for TSH, T3, T4?", a: "Primary: ↑TSH, ↓T3, ↓T4 (Hashimoto, iodine deficiency) - TSH is high because T3/T4 are not giving negative feedback. Secondary: ↓TSH, ↓T3, ↓T4, typically Sheehan syndrome, which the NBME has assessed with decreased TSH as well as decreased prolactin." },
  { id: "ar36", category: "Thyroid", q: "33F with exophthalmos, tremulousness, and type I diabetes - arrows for TSH, T3, T4, and radioiodine uptake?", a: "↓TSH, ↑T3, ↑T4, ↑131-I uptake. Graves; exophthalmos is seen ONLY in Graves. Thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin is structurally similar to TSH and agonizes the TSH receptor (type II hypersensitivity). The type I diabetes is there because autoimmune diseases go together; per Mehlman, do not overthink HLA associations - they are loose on the USMLE and will cost you questions." },
  { id: "ar37", category: "Thyroid", q: "How does radioiodine uptake sort out the causes of hyperthyroidism?", a: "Diffusely ↑uptake = Graves. Increased in ONE area = toxic adenoma. Multiple areas in an elderly patient = toxic multinodular goiter. Hyperthyroid with ↓uptake = thyroiditis (drug-induced, deQuervain, or postpartum) - the inflamed gland is releasing stored hormone, not producing it, which is why uptake is low." },
  { id: "ar38", category: "Thyroid", q: "19F with heavier longer periods, low mood, HR 60, high AST, high total cholesterol, high CK, and a mom with pernicious anemia - arrows for TSH, T3, T4, and radioiodine uptake?", a: "↑TSH, ↓T3, ↓T4, ↓131-I uptake. Hashimoto (chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis) - mixed antibody (anti-thyroperoxidase/anti-microsomal, anti-thyroglobulin) and T-cell destruction; uptake is diffusely decreased or 'patchy'. Per Mehlman, do not lean on brittle hair, dry skin, constipation, and cold intolerance - the real tells are menstrual irregularity, bradycardia, depression, high AST, high cholesterol, and high CK." },
  { id: "ar39", category: "Thyroid", q: "Most common cause of hypothyroidism in the West vs worldwide?", a: "Hashimoto in Western countries; iodine deficiency worldwide." },
  { id: "ar40", category: "Thyroid", q: "24M tremulous, heat intolerant, HR 92, TENDER thyroid - arrows for TSH, T3, T4, radioiodine uptake?", a: "↓TSH, ↑T3, ↑T4, ↓131-I uptake. DeQuervain (subacute granulomatous thyroiditis) after a viral infection: inflammation widens the spacing between cells and pre-formed hormone leaks out, so the gland is not overproducing and uptake is LOW. Per Mehlman this is one of the most commonly missed questions on the USMLE. Decreased uptake applies to ALL thyroiditis - deQuervain, drug-induced, and postpartum." },
  { id: "ar41", category: "Thyroid", q: "40F with a thyroid nodule whose biopsy shows apple-green birefringent amyloid - arrows for TSH, T3, T4, uptake?", a: "Either all ↔, or ↑TSH with ↓T3, T4, and uptake. Medullary thyroid carcinoma, with elevated calcitonin. Thyroid CARCINOMAS (papillary, follicular, medullary, anaplastic) are euthyroid or hypothyroid, never hyperthyroid. A nodule PLUS hyperthyroidism means toxic adenoma, which is benign." },
  { id: "ar42", category: "Thyroid", q: "How do you work up a thyroid nodule?", a: "If TSH is normal or high (euthyroid or hypothyroid): ultrasound before fine-needle aspiration - this is on the new 2CK Free 120, so ultrasound beats FNA when both are listed; if ultrasound is not listed, pick FNA. If TSH is LOW (hyperthyroid): radioiodine uptake scan, not ultrasound, because carcinomas do not secrete hormone - you are just sorting Graves (diffuse) from toxic adenoma (single focus) from toxic multinodular goiter (multifocal). On 2CK FM, palpation of the thyroid comes before checking TSH if the patient asks for cancer screening." },
  { id: "ar43", category: "Thyroid", q: "Patient is injecting T3 - arrows for TSH, T3, T4, radioiodine uptake?", a: "↓TSH, ↑T3, ↓T4, ↓uptake. Factitious thyrotoxicosis. T4 converts to T3 but T3 does NOT convert to T4, so injecting T3 suppresses TSH, understimulates the gland (small, non-palpable, low uptake), and T4 production falls. This combination is on a retired Step 1 NBME." },
  { id: "ar44", category: "Thyroid", q: "Patient is injecting levothyroxine (T4) - arrows for TSH, T3, T4, uptake?", a: "↓TSH, ↑T3, ↑T4, ↓uptake. T4 converts to T3, so both are high; that suppresses TSH and drops iodine uptake. You must specify T3 vs T4 injection - the T4 arrows differ from T3 injection." },
  { id: "ar45", category: "Thyroid", q: "If low TSH lowers uptake, why is uptake HIGH in Graves and toxic adenoma?", a: "In Graves, TSI mimics TSH and stimulates the gland directly. In toxic adenoma and toxic multinodular goiter, the tumorous tissue autonomously produces hormone without needing TSH. In factitious thyrotoxicosis there is nothing stimulating the gland, so uptake falls." },
  { id: "ar46", category: "Thyroid", q: "Subclinical hypothyroidism - arrows for TSH, T3, T4, and when do you treat?", a: "↑TSH, ↔T3, ↔T4, patient asymptomatic. Most do not need treatment; Step 3 wants treatment if TSH >10, the patient is pregnant, or anti-Hashimoto antibodies are positive." },
  { id: "ar47", category: "Thyroid", q: "54M recovering from ARDS being weaned from a ventilator - arrows for TSH, T4, T3, rT3?", a: "↔TSH, ↔T4, ↓T3, ↑reverse T3. Euthyroid sick syndrome - the vignette is a ventilator wean, major trauma, or recent surgery. Cortisol and inflammatory mediators inhibit peripheral T4 -> T3 conversion, and T4 is shunted to inactive reverse T3. Do not confuse it with subclinical hypothyroidism (↑TSH, unchanged T3 and T4)." },
  { id: "ar48", category: "Thyroid", q: "25F at 10 weeks of an uneventful pregnancy - arrows for TSH, T3, free T4, total T4, free/total T4 ratio?", a: "↔TSH, ↔T3, ↔free T4, ↑total T4, ↓free/total T4 ratio. Estrogen raises hepatic thyroid-binding globulin. TBG mops up free T4 -> TSH transiently rises -> the gland makes more T4 -> free T4 returns to normal while TBG-bound T4 is higher, so total T4 is up and the free/total ratio is down. T3 is normal because free T4 is normal." },
  { id: "ar49", category: "Thyroid", q: "2M with thyroid-binding globulin DEFICIENCY - arrows for TSH, T3, free T4, total T4, free/total ratio?", a: "↔TSH, ↔T3, ↔free T4, ↓total T4, ↑free/total T4 ratio. It is pregnancy in reverse - low TBG means less bound T4, so total T4 falls while free T4 stays normal. They ask this because the pregnancy arrows are so high-yield." },
  { id: "ar50", category: "Thyroid", q: "25F who gave birth 8 weeks ago with 4 days of tremulousness, tachycardia, and heat intolerance - arrows for TSH, T3, T4, uptake?", a: "↓TSH, ↑T3, ↑T4, ↓131-I uptake. Postpartum thyroiditis - it can present hypo- or hyperthyroid, and like deQuervain and drug-induced thyroiditis, uptake is LOW even when the patient is hyperthyroid. These arrows have nothing to do with the TBG changes of pregnancy." },
  { id: "ar51", category: "Thyroid", q: "40F on lithium with increased thirst and heat intolerance - arrows for TSH, T3, T4, uptake, MCD tonicity, and ADH?", a: "↓TSH, ↑T3, ↑T4, ↓131-I uptake, ↓(hypotonic) MCD, ↑ADH. Lithium causes drug-induced thyroiditis (low uptake even when hyperthyroid) AND nephrogenic DI. In nephrogenic DI ADH is HIGH because the hypothalamus and posterior pituitary work fine and compensate for renal insensitivity; in central DI ADH is low." },
  { id: "ar52", category: "Parathyroid", q: "Primary hypo- vs hyperparathyroidism - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH?", a: "Primary HYPOparathyroidism: ↓Ca, ↑PO4, ↓PTH - classically thyroid surgery damaging the parathyroids, or DiGeorge. Primary HYPERparathyroidism: ↑Ca, ↓PO4, ↑PTH - usually a PTH-secreting adenoma, sometimes four-gland hyperplasia." },
  { id: "ar53", category: "Parathyroid", q: "The three ways PTH raises calcium, and how it lowers phosphate?", a: "1) Bone: PTH binds osteoblasts -> they express RANK-L -> binds RANK on osteoclasts -> bone resorption releases calcium. 2) Kidney: increases an apical calcium channel in the late DCT for reabsorption. 3) Upregulates 1-alpha-hydroxylase in the PCT, converting 25-OH-D3 to 1,25-(OH)2-D3, which absorbs calcium AND phosphate in the small bowel. Phosphate falls because PTH downregulates apical PCT phosphate transporters; with low PTH those transporters stay up, phosphate is reabsorbed, and serum PO4 rises." },
  { id: "ar54", category: "MEN", q: "24M with 2 hours of shooting groin pain and a family history of thyroid cancer - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH, TSH?", a: "↑Ca, ↓PO4, ↑PTH, ↔TSH. MEN 2A. Shooting flank or groin pain = urolithiasis from hypercalcemia; the family thyroid cancer is medullary carcinoma (calcitonin high), and thyroid carcinoma patients are euthyroid or hypothyroid." },
  { id: "ar55", category: "MEN", q: "MEN 1, 2A, and 2B - what is in each?", a: "MEN 1: parathyroid adenoma/hyperplasia, pituitary adenoma, pancreatic adenoma. MEN 2A: parathyroid adenoma/hyperplasia, pheochromocytoma, medullary thyroid carcinoma. MEN 2B: Marfanoid habitus, mucosal neuromas, pheochromocytoma, medullary thyroid carcinoma." },
  { id: "ar56", category: "Parathyroid", q: "25F one week after total thyroidectomy with masseter twitching on stimulation - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH?", a: "↓Ca, ↑PO4, ↓PTH. Thyroidectomy can remove or damage the parathyroids. That masseter twitch is Chvostek sign; Trousseau sign of hypocalcemia is carpopedal spasm with BP cuff inflation. Low calcium gives 'up' findings (tetany, hyperreflexia); high calcium gives 'down' findings (flaccidity, hyporeflexia)." },
  { id: "ar57", category: "Parathyroid", q: "Trousseau sign of hypocalcemia vs Trousseau and Troisier signs of malignancy?", a: "Trousseau of HYPOCALCEMIA: carpopedal spasm with BP cuff inflation. Trousseau of MALIGNANCY: migratory thrombophlebitis, classically head-of-pancreas adenocarcinoma. Troisier: palpable left supraclavicular (Virchow) node in visceral malignancy." },
  { id: "ar58", category: "Parathyroid", q: "44M alcoholic with calcium 7.8 that only rises to 7.9 after IV calcium gluconate - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH, Mg2+?", a: "↓Ca, ↑PO4, ↓PTH, ↓Mg. Alcoholics get hypomagnesemia from poor intake, and low magnesium causes hypocalcemia and hypokalemia that do not respond to supplementation, because basal magnesium is required for the parathyroid gland to secrete PTH. On Step 2 the next best step is 'check serum magnesium.'" },
  { id: "ar59", category: "MEN", q: "47M with 5 years of recurrent duodenal ulcers, impotence, and reduced reflexes - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH?", a: "↑Ca, ↓PO4, ↑PTH. MEN 1: recurrent duodenal (or jejunal/ileal) ulcers means Zollinger-Ellison gastrinoma, impotence is how prolactinoma often presents in men, and hypercalcemia causes reduced reflexes and weakness." },
  { id: "ar60", category: "Immuno", q: "2M with cleft lip, absent thymic shadow, recurrent viral and Pneumocystis infections, and a harsh systolic murmur - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH, T cells, B cells?", a: "↓Ca, ↑PO4, ↓PTH, ↓T cells, ↔B cells. DiGeorge from 22q11 deletion - agenesis of the 3rd pouch (thymus + two inferior parathyroids) and 4th pouch (two superior parathyroids). Tetralogy of Fallot and truncus arteriosus are the common heart defects." },
  { id: "ar61", category: "Immuno", q: "Absent thymic shadow vs scanty lymph nodes and tonsils vs both?", a: "Absent thymic shadow = T cell deficiency = DiGeorge. Scanty lymph nodes/tonsils = B cell deficiency = Bruton agammaglobulinemia. Both together = SCID." },
  { id: "ar62", category: "Parathyroid", q: "66F with long-standing type II diabetes, creatinine 4.3, BUN 67 - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH?", a: "↓Ca, ↑PO4, ↑PTH. Secondary hyperparathyroidism from renal failure. Calcium is low for two reasons: the kidney cannot reabsorb it in the late DCT, and it cannot make 1,25-D3 in the PCT for gut absorption. Low calcium releases the calcium-sensing receptor brake, so PTH rises. Phosphate is HIGH despite the high PTH because the failing kidney cannot downregulate PCT phosphate transporters - the Ca and PO4 arrows are the opposite of primary hyperparathyroidism." },
  { id: "ar63", category: "Acid-Base", q: "64M with acute tubular necrosis - arrows for HCO3-, pH, CO2, anion gap?", a: "↓HCO3-, ↓pH, ↓CO2, ↑anion gap. Acute and chronic renal failure both cause high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, from reduced PCT bicarb reabsorption plus reduced proton secretion in the cortical collecting duct. Anion gap = Na - (Cl + HCO3); >12 is high. MUDPILES: Methanol, Uremia, DKA, Phenformin, Iron/INH, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates." },
  { id: "ar64", category: "Renal Failure", q: "70M with chronic renal failure - arrows for K+, PO4, HCO3-?", a: "↑K+, ↑PO4, ↓HCO3-. Potassium is high from impaired distal secretion (normally aldosterone's job), phosphate is high because PCT reabsorption cannot be downregulated, bicarb is low from the metabolic acidosis. Sodium is variable in renal failure." },
  { id: "ar65", category: "Vitamin D", q: "50F with renal insufficiency from granulomatosis with polyangiitis - arrows for 24,25-(OH)2-D3, fecal Ca2+, 1,25-(OH)2-D3?", a: "↑24,25-(OH)2-D3, ↑fecal Ca2+, ↓1,25-(OH)2-D3. The failing PCT cannot run 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so 25-D3 backs up and is shunted to the inactive storage form 24,25-D3 (treat 25-D3 and 24,25-D3 as moving together, but only 25-D3 converts to 1,25-D3). Less 1,25-D3 means less gut calcium absorption, so more calcium leaves in the feces." },
  { id: "ar66", category: "Parathyroid", q: "35F with a parathyroid adenoma - arrows for urinary Ca2+, serum Ca2+, urinary cAMP?", a: "↑urinary Ca2+, ↑serum Ca2+, ↑urinary cAMP. Not an erratum: PTH does reabsorb calcium in the late DCT, but serum calcium is so high that far more is filtered at the glomerulus to begin with, so urinary calcium still ends up high. Urinary cAMP rises because PTH signals through G-alpha-s -> adenylyl cyclase -> cAMP." },
  { id: "ar67", category: "Parathyroid", q: "22M with a calcium-sensing receptor mutation causing reduced receptor sensitivity - arrows for serum Ca2+ and PTH?", a: "↑serum Ca2+, ↑PTH. The calcium-sensing receptors on the parathyroids are negative-feedback receptors; blunting them means PTH is not suppressed by high calcium, so PTH stays inappropriately high and drives calcium up." },
  { id: "ar68", category: "Parathyroid", q: "24M with familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia - arrows for serum Ca2+, urinary Ca2+, parathyroid Ca-sensing receptor sensitivity, renal Ca-sensing receptor sensitivity?", a: "↑serum Ca2+, ↓urinary Ca2+, ↓parathyroid receptor sensitivity, ↓renal receptor sensitivity. Slightly (not fully) blunted receptors at both sites reset the calcium setpoint higher. PTH is high-normal or slightly up, patients are asymptomatic, and there is no bone resorption. The kidney tubules cannot sense the calcium, so they assume it is low and reabsorb more in the PCT and loop - the opposite of primary hyperparathyroidism, where normal renal sensors cut paracellular reabsorption and allow calciuresis." },
  { id: "ar69", category: "Parathyroid", q: "30F with a parathyroid adenoma given cinacalcet - arrows for serum Ca2+, serum PO4, urinary Ca2+, PTH?", a: "↓serum Ca2+, ↑serum PO4, ↓urinary Ca2+, ↓PTH. Cinacalcet is a calcimimetic that agonizes the parathyroid calcium-sensing receptors and suppresses PTH. Urinary calcium falls with serum calcium, because less is filtered at the glomerulus once normocalcemia returns." },
  { id: "ar70", category: "Parathyroid", q: "22M with forearm bony nodules, round cheeks, and short 4th and 5th metacarpals given exogenous PTH - arrows for serum Ca2+, serum PO4, urinary cAMP?", a: "↔serum Ca2+, ↔serum PO4, ↔urinary cAMP. Pseudohypoparathyroidism type I - a defective PTH receptor, so there is no response to exogenous PTH. The phenotype is Albright hereditary osteodystrophy: short 4th and 5th metacarpals, round face, bony abnormalities, and osteoma cutis. Normally exogenous PTH would raise calcium, lower phosphate, and raise urinary cAMP." },
  { id: "ar71", category: "Parathyroid", q: "16F with Albright hereditary osteodystrophy features - arrows for serum Ca2+, urinary Ca2+, serum PO4, PTH?", a: "↓serum Ca2+, ↓urinary Ca2+, ↑serum PO4, ↑PTH. Pseudohypoparathyroidism type I: receptor insensitivity makes the labs look like low PTH, while PTH itself is high from loss of negative feedback. Urinary calcium is low because serum calcium is low, so less is filtered to begin with." },
  { id: "ar72", category: "Parathyroid", q: "70F with chronic renal failure and TERTIARY hyperparathyroidism - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH?", a: "↑Ca, ↑PO4, ↑PTH. Long-standing secondary hyperparathyroidism causes parathyroid hyperplasia, so the glands autonomously oversecrete even after the calcium normalizes - the setpoint is reset. HIGH calcium in a renal failure patient is the red flag, since renal failure almost always gives LOW calcium. Phosphate stays high because phosphate is always high in renal failure." },
  { id: "ar73", category: "Vitamin D", q: "9M with alopecia and an inactivating vitamin D receptor mutation - arrows for serum Ca2+, PTH, 24,25-(OH)2-D3?", a: "↓serum Ca2+, ↑PTH, ↓24,25-(OH)2-D3 (the NBME calls it undetectable). No vitamin D action means no gut calcium absorption, so calcium falls and PTH rises. High PTH drives 1-alpha-hydroxylase, so little 25-D3 is left to be shunted into 24,25-D3 - and if asked for plain 25-OH-D3, pick a DOWN arrow. The NBME gives normal 1,25-D3." },
  { id: "ar74", category: "Paraneoplastic", q: "50M with a 60-pack-year history, 10 kg weight loss, a recent kidney stone, and a coin lesion on CXR - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH?", a: "↑Ca, ↓PO4, ↓PTH. Squamous cell carcinoma of the lung secreting PTH-related peptide. PTHrp acts like PTH (high calcium, low phosphate), but it is NOT PTH - endogenous PTH is suppressed by the hypercalcemia. Lung cancer + high calcium + LOW PTH = squamous cell." },
  { id: "ar75", category: "Paraneoplastic", q: "58F with hematuria, left flank pain, 10 kg weight loss, headaches and blurry vision, and a heterogeneous renal mass - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH, hemoglobin?", a: "↑Ca, ↓PO4, ↓PTH, ↑hemoglobin. Renal cell carcinoma secretes PTHrp (high calcium, low phosphate, suppressed PTH) and erythropoietin, causing secondary polycythemia and hyperviscosity - headache, blurry vision, Raynaud. Smoker + flank pain + red urine + high calcium + high hematocrit = RCC." },
  { id: "ar76", category: "Bone", q: "6F in the 10th percentile with bowing tibias whose family recently immigrated from Ireland - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH?", a: "↓Ca, ↓PO4, ↑PTH. Rickets (osteomalacia in adults) from vitamin D deficiency - vitamin D absorbs BOTH calcium and phosphate in the gut, and low calcium drives PTH up." },
  { id: "ar77", category: "Bone", q: "Low vitamin D with vs without renal failure - what is the phosphate arrow?", a: "Renal failure + low vitamin D: ↓Ca, ↑PO4, ↑PTH. No renal failure + low vitamin D (rickets/osteomalacia): ↓Ca, ↓PO4, ↑PTH. Never choose low phosphate in renal failure - the kidney cannot downregulate the PCT phosphate channels, and that wins over the vitamin D effect." },
  { id: "ar78", category: "Bone", q: "77F with bone density >2.5 SD below the mean - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH, 1,25-(OH)2-D3?", a: "ALL ↔. Osteoporosis has completely normal labs. Students assume calcium must be low; it is not. The USMLE often draws this as a graph with calcium on the x-axis and PTH on the y-axis - pick the letter in the normal range for both." },
  { id: "ar79", category: "Bone", q: "68F with osteoporosis - arrows for osteoblast activity, osteoclast activity, RANK-L activity?", a: "↓osteoblast activity, ↑osteoclast activity, ↑RANK-L activity. Bone cytokines (IL-1 and IL-6) stimulate RANK-L -> osteoclasts -> bone loss; estrogen normally suppresses those cytokines and preserves density. ALP reflects OSTEOBLAST activity: high in hyperparathyroidism and Paget, normal or low when osteoblast activity is low." },
  { id: "ar80", category: "Bone", q: "74M whose hat no longer fits, with mixed osteoblastic and osteoclastic occipital areas on head CT - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH, ALP?", a: "↔Ca, ↔PO4, ↔PTH, ↑ALP. Paget disease of bone - increased turnover causes an ISOLATED ALP elevation in 9 out of 10 questions. On 2CK the next best step is to check serum ALP. Paget can cause high-output cardiac failure with an S3 and a HIGH ejection fraction, from AV fistulae within bone." },
  { id: "ar81", category: "Bone", q: "68F with metastatic breast cancer - arrows for serum Ca2+ and PTH?", a: "↑Ca, ↓PTH. Bony metastases drive cytokine-mediated lysis, releasing calcium; the hypercalcemia suppresses PTH. This is not limited to breast cancer." },
  { id: "ar82", category: "Bone", q: "67M with mid-back pain, an IgG M spike, nephrotic syndrome, Congo red apple-green birefringence on renal biopsy, and an S4 - arrows for Ca2+, PTH, ESR?", a: "↑Ca, ↓PTH, ↑ESR. Multiple myeloma: lytic lesions raise calcium and suppress PTH; immunoglobulins coat RBCs making them sticky, giving rouleaux and a high ESR. Light chains show up in urine as Bence-Jones protein and deposit in the kidney (renal amyloidosis, nephrotic) and heart (restrictive cardiomyopathy, S4 from a stiff LV). Hypercalcemia from lytic lesions comes long before renal failure would drive calcium down." },
  { id: "ar83", category: "Bone", q: "32F African-American with chronic dry cough, red shins, and bihilar lymphadenopathy - arrows for Ca2+, PO4, PTH?", a: "↑Ca, ↑PO4, ↓PTH. Sarcoidosis, classically African-American women in their 20s-40s, with hypervitaminosis D. High vitamin D in real life almost always means granulomatous disease, not pill overdose." },
  { id: "ar84", category: "Vitamin D", q: "How does sarcoidosis raise calcium?", a: "Biopsy shows non-caseating granulomas whose activated (epithelioid) macrophages secrete 1-alpha-hydroxylase, converting inactive 25-D3 into active 1,25-D3 without needing PTH. Calcitriol then absorbs calcium and phosphate in the small bowel, and the hypercalcemia suppresses PTH. Very high-yield." },
  { id: "ar85", category: "Vitamin D", q: "35F with chronic dry cough, fever, and bihilar lymphadenopathy - arrows for fecal calcium, 24,25-(OH)2-D3, 1,25-(OH)2-D3?", a: "↓fecal calcium, ↓24,25-D3, ↑1,25-D3. Sarcoidosis: granuloma 1-alpha-hydroxylase raises calcitriol, so more calcium is absorbed and less ends up in the stool. 25-D3 is in equilibrium with the inactive storage form 24,25-D3, so shunting 25-D3 into 1,25-D3 leaves less for 24,25-D3." },
  { id: "ar86", category: "Vitamin D", q: "40M with dry cough, erythema nodosum, anemia, and bihilar lymphadenopathy - arrows for renal PCT 1-alpha-hydroxylase, 25-OH-D3, PTH?", a: "↓renal PCT 1-alpha-hydroxylase, ↓25-OH-D3, ↓PTH. Sarcoidosis: the LUNG granulomas make their own 1-alpha-hydroxylase, calcium rises, PTH is suppressed, and since PTH is what upregulates the RENAL enzyme, renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity falls. 25-D3 is low because more is being converted to 1,25-D3." },
  { id: "ar87", category: "Bone", q: "24F with a parathyroid adenoma - arrows for osteoblast activity, osteoclast activity, RANK-L expression, fecal calcium?", a: "↑osteoblast activity, ↑osteoclast activity, ↑RANK-L, ↓fecal calcium. PTH binds OSTEOBLASTS, not osteoclasts; the osteoblasts express RANK-L, which activates osteoclasts to resorb bone. NBME forms accept either 'stimulation of osteoblasts' or 'increased maturation and activation of osteoclasts,' so know the mechanism and stay flexible. Fecal calcium falls because PTH activates 1,25-D3 and the gut absorbs more calcium." },
  { id: "ar88", category: "Vitamin D", q: "6F with cystic fibrosis and steatorrhea - arrows for bone osteoid, bone hydroxyapatite, serum calcium, serum phosphate, PTH?", a: "↑osteoid, ↓hydroxyapatite, ↓calcium, ↓phosphate, ↑PTH. CF causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency -> fat malabsorption -> low fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K - All Dogs Eat Kittens). Vitamin D mineralizes osteoid into hydroxyapatite, so deficiency leaves unmineralized osteoid piled up." },
  { id: "ar89", category: "Vitamin D", q: "45M construction worker, chronic alcoholic, eats lots of fish and milk - arrows for hepatic hydroxylase, renal hydroxylase, 25-OH-D3, 1,25-(OH)2-D3?", a: "↓hepatic hydroxylase, ↑renal hydroxylase, ↓25-OH-D3, ↓1,25-D3. He gets plenty of sun, so skin synthesis is fine, but alcohol wrecks hepatic hydroxylation -> low 25-D3 -> low 1,25-D3 despite a calcium-rich diet -> PTH rises -> renal 1-alpha-hydroxylase activity goes UP trying to compensate. On the USMLE the answer to a calcium/vitamin D question in an alcoholic is often just 'decreased hepatic hydroxylase activity.'" },
  { id: "ar90", category: "Vitamin D", q: "40F office worker who never gets sun but eats lots of fish - arrows for 7-dehydrocholesterol, cholecalciferol, 25-OH-D3, 1,25-(OH)2-D3?", a: "↑7-dehydrocholesterol, ↓cholecalciferol, ↓25-OH-D3, ↓1,25-D3. UV-B converts 7-dehydrocholesterol in the stratum basale into cholecalciferol; no sun means the precursor backs up and everything downstream falls. If asked whose synthesis is decreased with low sun exposure, the answer is CHOLECALCIFEROL, not 7-dehydrocholesterol." },
  { id: "ar91", category: "Repro", q: "54F with hot flashes and a year of irregular menses - arrows for estrogen, inhibin B, LH, FSH?", a: "↓estrogen, ↓inhibin B, ↑LH, ↑FSH. Menopause: the ovaries stop making estrogen and inhibin B, so negative feedback falls and GnRH, LH, and FSH rise. Premature ovarian failure (menopause at 40 or younger) gives the same arrows." },
  { id: "ar92", category: "Repro", q: "What do LH and FSH do in the ovary, and what are the male equivalents?", a: "LH activates theca interna cells to make androgens. FSH activates granulosa cells to make aromatase and inhibin B, and that aromatase converts theca androgens into estrogens. In males, Leydig cells are the theca interna equivalent and Sertoli cells are the granulosa equivalent." },
  { id: "ar93", category: "Repro", q: "14F who has never menstruated, height 4'10\", Tanner stage 1 breasts - arrows for estrogen, inhibin B, LH, FSH?", a: "↓estrogen, ↓inhibin, ↑LH, ↑FSH. Turner syndrome (45XO) - streak ovaries, 'menopause before menarche,' short stature, webbed neck (cystic hygroma), shield chest, aortic coarctation, bicuspid aortic valve, scattered nevi." },
  { id: "ar94", category: "Repro", q: "18F cross country runner, BMI 16, no period for 4 months - arrows for estrogen, inhibin B, GnRH, LH, FSH?", a: "ALL FIVE ↓. Anorexia: low BMI means low leptin, and leptin is required for normal GnRH pulsatility, so GnRH falls and takes LH and FSH with it - hypogonadotropic (central) amenorrhea. Low LH means low theca androgens; low FSH means low inhibin B and low aromatase; adipose aromatase is also reduced, so estrogen is low. The gonadotropins do NOT rise despite low negative feedback, because the problem is central." },
  { id: "ar95", category: "Repro", q: "The bottom-line estrogen/FSH split for amenorrhea?", a: "Menopause, premature ovarian failure, and Turner: ↓estrogen with ↑FSH. Anorexia: ↓estrogen with ↓FSH." },
  { id: "ar96", category: "Repro", q: "22M, 6'2\", eunuchoid habitus, Barr body on EM of somatic cells - arrows for testosterone, estrogen, LH, FSH?", a: "↓testosterone, ↑estrogen, ↑LH, ↑FSH. Klinefelter (47XXY): seminiferous tubule dysgenesis drops testosterone, so negative feedback falls and GnRH/LH/FSH rise. Estrogen is high from overexpression of aromatase (CYP19) in the testes." },
  { id: "ar97", category: "Repro", q: "24F with occasional missed periods, acne, BMI 27 - arrows for testosterone, LH, FSH, luteal progesterone?", a: "↑testosterone, ↑LH, ↓FSH, ↓luteal progesterone. PCOS: high BMI and insulin resistance cause abnormal GnRH pulsation with a high LH/FSH ratio. If asked for FSH separately, choose DOWN, not up. Low FSH means the follicle is not mature when the LH surge comes, so no ovulation, no corpus luteum, no progesterone - and menstruation requires progesterone to be present and then withdrawn." },
  { id: "ar98", category: "Repro", q: "Why does PCOS raise endometrial cancer risk?", a: "Unopposed estrogen. Estrogen proliferates endometrium and progesterone limits it; with chronic anovulation, lifetime progesterone exposure is low, so hyperplasia and atypia are more likely. PCOS also causes subfertility." },
  { id: "ar99", category: "Repro", q: "28F with missed periods, acne, BMI 27 - arrows for testosterone, insulin, FSH?", a: "↑testosterone, ↑insulin, ↓FSH. PCOS. High BMI -> insulin resistance -> hyperinsulinemia -> abnormal GnRH pulsation with a high LH/FSH ratio. Sometimes the answer to 'what would you most likely see' in a PCOS vignette is simply hyperinsulinemia." },
  { id: "ar100", category: "Repro", q: "22M bodybuilder with large muscle bulk and acne on face and back - arrows for serum LH and intratesticular testosterone?", a: "↓serum LH, ↓intratesticular testosterone. Exogenous androgens suppress pituitary LH by negative feedback; LH normally drives Leydig cells, so intratesticular production falls even though SERUM testosterone is high." },
  { id: "ar101", category: "Repro", q: "16F who has never menstruated, vagina ends in a blind pouch, Tanner 4 breasts, scanty pubic and axillary hair - arrows for LH and testosterone?", a: "↑LH, ↑testosterone. Androgen insensitivity syndrome (46XY). Insensitive androgen receptors mean less negative feedback, so LH rises and drives more testosterone. Same vignette with NORMAL coarse pubic/axillary hair = Mullerian agenesis (46XX)." },
  { id: "ar102", category: "Repro", q: "12F who grew 4 inches in 4 months with upper lip hair and a 3 cm clitoral hood - arrows for testosterone, 5-alpha-reductase, DHT, phosphodiesterase 5?", a: "↑testosterone, ↓5-alpha-reductase, ↓DHT, ↔phosphodiesterase 5. 5-alpha-reductase deficiency ('phallus at age 12'): karyotypically male but phenotypically female until puberty, when rising testosterone partially overcomes the enzyme deficiency and virilization begins. It can also be given as a 46XY neonate with ambiguous genitalia. PDE5 is unrelated - that is the sildenafil target, which blocks cGMP breakdown to allow erection." },
  { id: "ar103", category: "Pituitary", q: "30M with a year of impotence, milky nipple discharge, and peripheral vision loss - arrows for prolactin, GnRH, estrogen?", a: "↑prolactin, ↓GnRH, ↓estrogen. Prolactinoma - impotence and galactorrhea in men, amenorrhea in women. Prolactin suppresses GnRH pulsation, so sex hormones fall. Treat with bromocriptine (D2 agonist), since dopamine inhibits prolactin; antipsychotics and metoclopramide (D2 antagonists) cause hyperprolactinemia. Thiazides can cause galactorrhea (offline NBME 20)." },
  { id: "ar104", category: "Pituitary", q: "16M whose pituitary stalk is severed in a car accident - arrows for LH, FSH, ACTH, GH, TSH, prolactin?", a: "↓LH, FSH, ACTH, GH, and TSH, but ↑prolactin. The stalk carries hypothalamic signals: GnRH -> LH/FSH, CRH -> ACTH, GHRH -> GH, TRH -> TSH, and dopamine INHIBITS prolactin. Cut the stalk and every stimulated hormone falls while prolactin loses its brake and rises." },
  { id: "ar105", category: "OB", q: "45F at 9 weeks whose CVS shows trisomy 21 - what would the tri-screen have shown for PAPP-A, beta-hCG, nuchal translucency?", a: "↓PAPP-A, ↑beta-hCG, ↑nuchal translucency. First trimester screen at 8-10 weeks. Nuchal translucency is fluid behind the fetal neck, thickened in Down. A hypoplastic nasal bone is not formally part of the screen but is suggestive (flattened facies)." },
  { id: "ar106", category: "OB", q: "45F at 17 weeks with a Down syndrome fetus - arrows for AFP, beta-hCG, estriol, inhibin A?", a: "↓AFP, ↑beta-hCG, ↓estriol, ↑inhibin A. Second trimester quad screen at 16-18 weeks. AFP is HIGH in neural tube defects, anencephaly, multiple gestation, and omphalocele/gastroschisis. Hook: the variables with an 'H' (beta-hCG, inhibin A) are the up arrows. Inhibin A is the quad screen marker; inhibin B is what granulosa and Sertoli cells secrete in response to FSH. In Edward syndrome all four are down, and the USMLE will not ask arrows for Patau." },
  { id: "ar107", category: "Acid-Base", q: "26F at 8 weeks vomiting every 3 hours for 48 hours - arrows for K+, Cl-, HCO3-, pH, CO2, urinary ketones?", a: "↓K+, ↓Cl-, ↑HCO3-, ↑pH, ↑CO2, ↑urinary ketones. Vomiting causes a hypokalemic, hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis: vomitus is rich in K, Cl, and protons, and volume depletion drives RAAS to dump more K and H distally. CO2 is retained as compensation. Urinary ketones are how you diagnose hyperemesis gravidarum; treat by admitting and giving IV antiemetics." },
  { id: "ar108", category: "OB", q: "Why does hCG peak at 8-10 weeks?", a: "hCG from syncytiotrophoblast maintains the corpus luteum so it keeps making progesterone to hold the endometrial lining. At 8-10 weeks the placenta takes over progesterone production, the corpus luteum is no longer needed, and hCG declines. On a graph of pregnancy hormones, hCG is the one that peaks at 8-10 weeks - and it is what drives first-trimester nausea and vomiting." },
  { id: "ar109", category: "Acid-Base", q: "18F using laxatives to lose weight - arrows for K+, Cl-, HCO3-, pH, CO2, anion gap?", a: "↓K+, ↑Cl-, ↓HCO3-, ↓pH, ↓CO2, ↔anion gap. Stool is rich in potassium and bicarb, so diarrhea and laxative abuse cause a NORMAL anion-gap metabolic acidosis (diarrhea is not in MUDPILES). Chloride rises from cellular shifts, and CO2 is blown off to compensate." },
  { id: "ar110", category: "Diuretics", q: "26F abusing furosemide to lose weight - arrows for K+, Cl-, HCO3-, pH, CO2?", a: "↓K+, ↓Cl-, ↑HCO3-, ↑pH, ↑CO2. Loops block the apical Na/K/2Cl symporter in the thick ascending limb; the volume loss upregulates RAAS, which secretes protons distally - contraction alkalosis. Thiazides follow the same pattern, and these are also the same arrows as vomiting." },
  { id: "ar111", category: "Diuretics", q: "25M abusing spironolactone - arrows for K+, HCO3-, pH, CO2, androgen receptor activity?", a: "↑K+, ↓HCO3-, ↓pH, ↓CO2, ↓androgen receptor activity. Aldosterone antagonists are potassium-sparing and block the apical H+-ATPase, giving a normal anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Spironolactone blocks androgen receptors and causes gynecomastia; eplerenone less so." },
  { id: "ar112", category: "Diuretics", q: "Which diuretic do you add to a loop, and why not a thiazide?", a: "A potassium-sparing diuretic - spironolactone, eplerenone, triamterene, or amiloride. Adding a thiazide after a loop drops potassium too far. Triamterene and amiloride block ENaC, which secondarily disables the basolateral Na/K-ATPase, so aldosterone's renal effects are impaired." },
  { id: "ar113", category: "Diuretics", q: "44M with essential hypertension started on hydrochlorothiazide - arrows for serum Ca2+, K+, HCO3-, pH, urinary calcium?", a: "↑serum Ca2+, ↓K+, ↑HCO3-, ↑pH, ↓urinary calcium. Thiazides block the Na/Cl symporter in the early DCT, which promotes distal calcium REABSORPTION - useful for recurrent calcium stones, and occasionally causing hypercalcemia. Volume depletion drives RAAS and distal proton and potassium loss. Loops do the OPPOSITE to calcium (low serum Ca, high urinary Ca) while causing the same hypokalemia and alkalosis." },
  { id: "ar114", category: "Renal Tubular", q: "20F with increased urination after taking expired tetracyclines - arrows for PCT reabsorption of amino acids, glucose, phosphate, bicarbonate?", a: "ALL ↓. Fanconi syndrome from expired tetracyclines; the bicarb part causes type II RTA. A retired Step 1 NBME has all four as down arrows. Do not confuse it with Fanconi ANEMIA, an autosomal recessive aplastic anemia with absent or hypoplastic thumbs and radii." },
  { id: "ar115", category: "Renal Tubular", q: "RTA types I, II, and IV?", a: "Type I: the DCT cannot secrete protons. Type II: the PCT cannot reabsorb bicarbonate. Type IV: aldosterone insensitivity at the kidney (the aldosterone version of nephrogenic DI) or hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism. All cause NORMAL anion-gap metabolic acidosis - they are not in MUDPILES." },
  { id: "ar116", category: "Renal Failure", q: "22F abusing diuretics with poor skin turgor, sunken orbits, dry mucous membranes, and high creatinine and BUN - arrows for BUN/Cr and FeNa?", a: "↑BUN/Cr, ↓FeNa. Prerenal azotemia (classic values BUN/Cr >20, FeNa <1%). Poor perfusion makes the kidney reabsorb more sodium and urea in the PCT so water follows - urea leaving the urine raises serum BUN, and sodium leaving the urine lowers FeNa. Other prerenal causes: left heart failure and NSAIDs." },
  { id: "ar117", category: "Renal Failure", q: "40F whose BP fell to 80/40 for 30 seconds during a hysterectomy and who is now oliguric - arrows for BUN/Cr and FeNa?", a: "↓BUN/Cr, ↑FeNa (BUN/Cr <20, FeNa >1%). This is acute tubular necrosis, which is INTRARENAL - students call it prerenal and get it wrong. An ABRUPT drop in perfusion (exsanguination, V-fib) kills the PCT first because it has the highest oxygen demand from all its ATPase pumps. Prerenal is for subacute or chronic drops: diuretics, dehydration, left heart failure, NSAIDs." },
  { id: "ar118", category: "Renal Failure", q: "Other causes of acute tubular necrosis and of postrenal failure?", a: "ATN: aminoglycosides, IV contrast, and rhabdomyolysis (myoglobin is nephrotoxic). Postrenal: an older man with BPH, a woman with cervical or ovarian cancer impinging the ureters, or posterior urethral valves in peds. Intra- and postrenal have lower BUN/Cr and higher FeNa because the kidney is not trying to reabsorb urea and sodium to defend volume." },
  { id: "ar119", category: "Diuretics", q: "30M going to the mountains prescribed acetazolamide - arrows for urinary volume, urinary bicarbonate, urine pH?", a: "ALL ↑. Carbonic anhydrase inhibition in the PCT means less H2CO3 formed, so less bicarb reabsorbed - and less Na and K reabsorbed with it, so water stays in the tubule and diuresis follows. Bicarb is basic, so urine pH rises. Careful: the SERUM arrows are the opposite - decreased volume, decreased bicarb, decreased pH." },
  { id: "ar120", category: "Acid-Base", q: "Why does acetazolamide help altitude sickness?", a: "At altitude the low O2 tension drives hyperventilation -> CO2 blown off -> acute respiratory alkalosis. The kidney needs 12-24 hours to excrete bicarb and compensate; acetazolamide forces that bicarb excretion faster." },
  { id: "ar121", category: "CAH", q: "4F with ambiguous genitalia, high 17-OH-progesterone, and normal BP - arrows for K+, Na+, pH, HCO3-, glucose?", a: "↑K+, ↓Na+, ↓pH, ↓HCO3-, ↓glucose. 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the classic congenital adrenal hyperplasia; cortisol, aldosterone, and their precursors are all low, and BP is low or normal. Per Mehlman, if you are just trying to pass, memorize these 21-hydroxylase arrows." },
  { id: "ar122", category: "CAH", q: "How do you tell 21-, 11-, and 17-hydroxylase deficiency apart?", a: "Both 21- AND 11- have ↑17-OH substrates (17-OH-progesterone, 17-OH-pregnenolone), ↑DHEA-S, and ↑androstenedione - see any of those high and it is one of those two. Then use potassium and BP: high K and/or low-normal BP = 21-hydroxylase; low-normal K and/or HIGH BP = 11-hydroxylase. If the 17-OH substrates, DHEA-S, and androstenedione are all LOW, it is 17-hydroxylase deficiency." },
  { id: "ar123", category: "CAH", q: "11-hydroxylase deficiency - arrows?", a: "↓or unchanged K+, ↑or unchanged Na+, pH, HCO3-, and glucose, with ↑BP. Cortisol and aldosterone are low, but their precursors build up and have enough mineralocorticoid effect to raise BP - which is what separates it from 21-hydroxylase deficiency." },
  { id: "ar124", category: "CAH", q: "17-hydroxylase deficiency - arrows?", a: "↓17-OH substrates, ↓DHEA-S, ↓androstenedione, ↓K+, ↑Na+, ↑pH, ↑HCO3- (from high aldosterone), and ↓glucose (cortisol and its precursor are low). BP is high." },
  { id: "ar125", category: "CAH", q: "4M with high BP and high serum DHEA-S - arrows for K+ and 17-OH-progesterone?", a: "↓K+, ↑17-OH-progesterone. High DHEA-S narrows it to 21- or 11-hydroxylase; the HIGH BP makes it 11-hydroxylase deficiency, so potassium is low or normal while the 17-OH substrates are elevated." },
  { id: "ar126", category: "CAH", q: "4M with high BP and LOW DHEA-S and 17-OH-pregnenolone - arrows for K+, Na+, pH, HCO3-, glucose?", a: "↓K+, ↑Na+, ↑pH, ↑HCO3-, ↓glucose. 17-hydroxylase deficiency: low 17-OH substrates and DHEA-S give it away, aldosterone is high (hypokalemia, hypernatremia, metabolic alkalosis, high BP), and glucose is low because glucocorticoids cannot be made." },
  { id: "ar127", category: "CAH", q: "4F with high potassium and high DHEA-S and normal BP - arrows for 17-, 11-, and 21-hydroxylase functionality?", a: "↔17-hydroxylase, ↔11-hydroxylase, ↓21-hydroxylase. High DHEA-S puts it at 21- or 11-; high potassium with normal BP makes it 21-hydroxylase deficiency." },
  { id: "ar128", category: "CAH", q: "6F with high BP and high 17-OH-progesterone - arrows for 17-, 11-, and 21-hydroxylase functionality?", a: "↔17-hydroxylase, ↓11-hydroxylase, ↔21-hydroxylase. High 17-OH substrates put it at 21- or 11-; the high BP makes it 11-hydroxylase deficiency." },
  { id: "ar129", category: "DKA", q: "11F with a day of lethargy after a week of polyuria and polydipsia following a viral illness - arrows for pH, HCO3-, CO2?", a: "ALL ↓. Diabetic ketoacidosis, classically after a viral infection (Coxsackie B). No insulin means cells cannot take up glucose, so gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and ketogenesis run wild; ketone bodies are acidic, so bicarb falls, and CO2 is blown off as compensation - that is Kussmaul breathing." },
  { id: "ar130", category: "DKA", q: "12F with type I diabetes who skipped evening insulin, now lethargic and delirious - arrows for beta-hydroxybutyrate, serum osmolality, anion gap?", a: "ALL ↑. Beta-hydroxybutyrate is a ketone body and has shown up as an arrow on an offline Step 1 NBME. Serum osmolality rises because glucose is markedly high with no insulin to move it into cells. DKA is the D in MUDPILES, so the anion gap - Na - (Cl + HCO3), high at >12 - is up." },
  { id: "ar131", category: "DKA", q: "8M with recent viral illness, lethargy, polyuria, polydipsia - arrows for serum Na+, serum K+, TOTAL body K+, GFR?", a: "↓serum Na+, ↑serum K+, ↓total body K+, ↑GFR. Sodium is diluted (high osmolality holds fluid intravascularly, and low ATP means less Na/K-ATPase pumping). Potassium is HIGH in serum but LOW in the body: acidosis drives the H+/K+ antiporter to push K out of cells, and absent insulin means K is not driven in - meanwhile the kidney keeps dumping the filtered potassium. GFR is up from hyperglycemic hyperfiltration." },
  { id: "ar132", category: "Toxicology", q: "3F who ate a bottle of pills, now with bloody stool and pills visible on abdominal x-ray - arrows for pH, HCO3-, CO2, anion gap?", a: "↓pH, ↓HCO3-, ↓CO2, ↑anion gap. Acute iron toxicity - iron tablets are the I in MUDPILES, and their metal content makes them visible on x-ray, which helps diagnose an infant ingestion." },
  { id: "ar133", category: "Toxicology", q: "25M who drank a bottle of aspirin 10 minutes ago - arrows for pH, HCO3-, CO2?", a: "↑pH, ↔HCO3-, ↓CO2. Acute respiratory alkalosis - aspirin directly stimulates the brain's respiratory centers in the first ~20 minutes. Bicarb is normal because the kidney needs 12-24 hours to change it, and that normal bicarb is exactly what makes this an ACUTE respiratory alkalosis." },
  { id: "ar134", category: "Toxicology", q: "The two-stage acid-base picture of salicylate toxicity?", a: "First 20 minutes: acute respiratory alkalosis - ↑pH, ↔HCO3-, ↓CO2. After 20 minutes: mixed metabolic acidosis and respiratory alkalosis - ↓pH, ↓HCO3-, ↓CO2. The low bicarb is NOT renal compensation (too fast for that); salicylic acid is itself an acid driving a metabolic acidosis that wins out." },
  { id: "ar135", category: "Toxicology", q: "20F who took a bottle of pills an hour ago with lethargy and ear ringing - arrows for pH, HCO3-, CO2, anion gap, Na+, K+?", a: "↓pH, ↓HCO3-, ↓CO2, ↑anion gap, ↔Na+, ↔K+. Salicylate toxicity - tinnitus is the usual first symptom, and this is the mixed metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis. Salicylates are the S in MUDPILES, so the calculated gap must be 13 or greater. Sodium and potassium being normal confuses students, but they are unchanged." },
  { id: "ar136", category: "Acid-Base", q: "38F 48 hours after a C-section with a sore left leg and sudden dyspnea and tachycardia - arrows for pH, HCO3-, CO2?", a: "↑pH, ↔HCO3-, ↓CO2. Pulmonary embolism causes acute respiratory alkalosis from tachypnea. CO2 diffuses quickly and O2 slowly, so even with bad lung pathology a hyperventilating patient blows off CO2 - true for 14 of 15 USMLE questions. Bicarb has not had the 12-24 hours it needs to change." },
  { id: "ar137", category: "Acid-Base", q: "12M wheezing on the soccer field for 10 minutes with a family history of eczema and allergies - arrows for pH, HCO3-, CO2?", a: "↑pH, ↔HCO3-, ↓CO2. Acute asthma attack with hyperventilation and acute respiratory alkalosis. Both O2 and CO2 are low: O2 diffuses slowly so it cannot get in, but CO2 diffuses fast so it still gets out." },
  { id: "ar138", category: "Acid-Base", q: "Same asthmatic 40 minutes after albuterol, O2 still low, respiratory rate 6 - arrows for pH, HCO3-, CO2?", a: "↓pH, ↔HCO3-, ↑CO2. Respiratory acidosis from respiratory fatigue - normal rate is 12-16, and he has tired out and slowed down despite low oxygen." },
  { id: "ar139", category: "Acid-Base", q: "The three stages of an asthma attack, and type I vs type II respiratory failure?", a: "1) High rate: ↑pH, unchanged HCO3-, ↓CO2 - low O2 with low CO2 is TYPE I respiratory failure (they move the same direction). 2) Tiring: pH, HCO3-, and CO2 all back to normal while O2 stays low - impending type II. 3) Fatigued: ↓pH, unchanged HCO3-, ↑CO2 - TYPE II respiratory failure, high CO2 with low O2 (opposite directions). Transitioning from type I to type II may require intubation." },
  { id: "ar140", category: "Acid-Base", q: "16F called to the principal's office who hyperventilates - arrows for pH, HCO3-, CO2, and cerebral perfusion?", a: "↑pH, ↔HCO3-, ↓CO2, ↓cerebral perfusion. Panic attack. Low CO2 lowers cerebral perfusion, which is why she may faint - exceedingly high-yield. There is an NBME question asking how an anesthesiologist can increase cerebral perfusion: decrease the respiratory rate to retain CO2. Hyperventilation is also the first step in managing raised intracranial pressure." },
  { id: "ar141", category: "Acid-Base", q: "72M with COPD, fever, labored breathing, respiratory rate 32 - arrows for pH, HCO3-, CO2?", a: "↓pH, ↑HCO3-, ↑CO2. Acute exacerbation of COPD is THE exception to the 'hyperventilation means low CO2' rule - this is acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis, with the high bicarb reflecting the chronic component." },
  { id: "ar142", category: "Acid-Base", q: "Why is CO2 still high in an acute COPD exacerbation even with a respiratory rate of 32?", a: "COPD (chronic bronchitis plus emphysema) destroys alveolar surface area, so CO2 cannot leave the capillaries no matter how fast the patient breathes. Baseline they are chronic CO2 retainers with chronic respiratory acidosis (↑CO2, ↑HCO3-, ↓pH), and the acute insult adds more CO2 that the chronic bicarb elevation cannot cover. A 2CK NBME gives rate 32 with CO2 of 80 and the answer is 'chronic respiratory acidosis and acute respiratory acidosis.'" },
  { id: "ar143", category: "Acid-Base", q: "68M with long-standing COPD and no acute distress - arrows for pH, HCO3-, CO2?", a: "↓pH, ↑HCO3-, ↑CO2. Chronic respiratory acidosis with renal compensation; pH may be normal or low depending on severity. Obstructive sleep apnea, obesity, severe ankylosing spondylitis, and severe kyphoscoliosis give the same numbers through restriction, but those patients CAN blow off CO2 by hyperventilating." },
  { id: "ar144", category: "A-a Gradient", q: "60F after hip surgery on morphine, respiratory rate 8 - arrows for CO2 and A-a gradient?", a: "↑CO2, ↔A-a gradient. Opioids, barbiturates, and benzodiazepines depress respiration. The A-a gradient is NORMAL because both alveolar and arterial oxygen are low - she is simply not moving air. Contrast pulmonary fibrosis, where alveolar oxygen is fine but cannot cross, so the gradient is high. The USMLE just offers you something like 40 versus 8; pick 8 for hypoventilation." },
  { id: "ar145", category: "Cardio", q: "65M with hypertension, smoking, an S3, and 4-pillow orthopnea - arrows for A-a gradient and PCWP?", a: "BOTH ↑. Dilated cardiomyopathy with left heart failure; S3 means a dilated LV with systolic dysfunction. Lying flat increases venous return the failing left heart cannot handle, so blood backs into the lungs - hence orthopnea. Alveolar oxygen is fine but edema fluid blocks transfer, so the A-a gradient is high. PCWP equals left atrial pressure, and ANY left heart pathology raises it. A 2CK NBME asks the mechanism of dyspnea in pulmonary edema: increased alveolar-arteriolar oxygen gradient." },
  { id: "ar146", category: "Cardio", q: "8M with recurrent lung infections, failed to pass meconium, loud P2, RBBB, and JVD - arrows for PCWP and 24,25-(OH)2-D3?", a: "↔PCWP, ↓24,25-D3. Cor pulmonale from cystic fibrosis - right heart failure from a pulmonary cause, with a normal left heart, so PCWP (= left atrial pressure) is normal. Loud P2 means pulmonary hypertension; RBBB usually means right ventricular hypertrophy (LBBB means LVH). CF causes fat malabsorption -> low dietary cholecalciferol -> low 25-OH-D3 -> low 24,25-D3, which is in equilibrium with it." },
  { id: "ar147", category: "V/Q", q: "47M with cold-induced color change in the fingers, reflux, and a year of worsening dyspnea - arrows for V/Q, anti-centromere antibodies, anti-mitochondrial antibodies?", a: "↓V/Q, ↑anti-centromere, ↔/↓anti-mitochondrial. Pulmonary fibrosis from limited systemic sclerosis (CREST). Perfusion is fine but fibrosis under-ventilates alveoli, so V/Q is LOW. Anti-centromere is CREST; anti-Scl-70 (topoisomerase) is diffuse systemic sclerosis; anti-mitochondrial is primary biliary cirrhosis and is thrown in as a distractor." },
  { id: "ar148", category: "V/Q", q: "Shunt vs dead space, and the normal apex-to-base V/Q values?", a: "LOW V/Q = shunt, which is most lung pathology. HIGH V/Q = dead space, which is pulmonary embolism. Physiologically, both ventilation and perfusion rise from apex to base, but gravity pulls blood down more than air, so perfusion rises more: V/Q is about 0.6 at the bases (natural shunt) and about 3.0 at the apices (natural dead space)." },
  { id: "ar149", category: "V/Q", q: "40F heavy smoker on combined OCPs with sudden dyspnea and tachycardia - arrows for pH, CO2, bicarb, V/Q?", a: "↑pH, ↓CO2, ↔HCO3-, ↑V/Q. Pulmonary embolism - combined OCPs are contraindicated in smokers over 35 for exactly this reason. Acute respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation with no time for bicarb to move, and high V/Q because perfusion is blocked: pathologic dead space." },
  { id: "ar150", category: "V/Q", q: "Alveolar vs anatomic vs physiologic dead space?", a: "Alveolar dead space is the natural V/Q shift from apices to bases. Anatomic dead space is airway that is ventilated but never perfused, like the bronchi. Physiologic dead space = alveolar + anatomic. One retired Step 1 NBME lists all three plus shunt as answer choices." },
  { id: "ar151", category: "Pulm", q: "46M alcoholic with abdominal pain, calcium 7.5, glucose 200, severe hypoxemia, bilateral infiltrates - arrows for PCWP, V/Q, surfactant protein D?", a: "↔PCWP, ↓V/Q, ↑surfactant protein D. ARDS from pancreatitis. ARDS is always bilateral and non-cardiogenic, so PCWP is normal - high PCWP with bilateral infiltrates would mean left heart pathology instead. V/Q is low because this is a shunt. Surfactant protein D is a lung injury marker (retired Step 1 NBME)." },
  { id: "ar152", category: "GI", q: "Which pancreatitis labs carry prognosis, and why is calcium low?", a: "Low calcium and high glucose are the Ranson criteria worth knowing; amylase and lipase levels do NOT correlate with prognosis. Calcium falls through saponification - enzymatic fat necrosis chelates calcium." },
  { id: "ar153", category: "PFTs", q: "55M non-smoker with 6 months of dyspnea, dry cough, and honeycombing on CT - arrows for FEV1, FVC, FEV1/FVC?", a: "↓FEV1, ↓FVC, ↑FEV1/FVC. Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, a restrictive disease, also known as usual interstitial pneumonitis (UIP) - memorize that equivalence, it is an NBME answer. The ratio is normal or high because radial traction from fibrosis holds airways open, so FEV1 does not fall as much as FVC." },
  { id: "ar154", category: "PFTs", q: "70F healthy compared with a healthy 20-year-old - arrows for FEV1, FVC, FEV1/FVC?", a: "ALL ↓. People naturally develop obstructive physiology over a lifetime of inhaling particulates. Same arrows as COPD from smoking, asthma, and alpha-1-antitrypsin emphysema. Knowing the obstructive vs restrictive PFT arrows is mandatory to pass Step 1." },
  { id: "ar155", category: "PFTs", q: "25M non-smoker with worsening dyspnea and bullous changes on CXR whose dad died of alcoholic liver disease - arrows for residual volume, arterial pO2, A-a gradient?", a: "↑residual volume, ↓arterial pO2, ↑A-a gradient. Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency: pan-acinar emphysema plus cirrhosis, codominant inheritance (the father's allele made him more susceptible to alcoholic liver disease). Bullous changes = emphysema = obstructive. Residual volume is UP in obstructive and DOWN in restrictive; pO2 is down and A-a is up in both, plus in pulmonary edema. A-a stays NORMAL only in hypoventilation - benzos, barbiturates, opioids." },
  { id: "ar156", category: "Pulm", q: "Neonate born at 26 weeks with respiratory distress - arrows for pulmonary compliance, elastic recoil, sphingomyelin, lamellar body activity?", a: "↓compliance, ↑elastic recoil, ↑sphingomyelin, ↓lamellar body activity. Neonatal RDS from surfactant deficiency. Compliance is the ability to expand, elastic recoil the tendency to collapse; without surfactant, collapsing forces win. The lecithin:sphingomyelin ratio above 2.0-2.4 means mature lungs, so in NRDS lecithin is low and sphingomyelin high. Lamellar bodies are the surfactant-making organelles in type II pneumocytes." },
  { id: "ar157", category: "Pulm", q: "50M smoker with COPD - arrows for mucous production, airway cilia activity, alveolar macrophage function?", a: "↑mucous production (increased Reid index), ↓ciliary activity, ↓alveolar macrophage function. Both the cilia and the macrophages are IMPAIRED by particulates, not upregulated. The NBME asks which cell is most impaired in a smoker: the pseudostratified columnar epithelial cell." },
  { id: "ar158", category: "Cardio", q: "25F 10 miles into a run with nothing to drink - arrows for carotid sinus baroreceptor firing, vagus nerve firing, SA node sympathetic activity?", a: "↓baroreceptor firing, ↓vagal firing, ↑sympathetic activity. Baroreceptor firing is STRETCH-dependent: low BP or dehydration means LOW firing, not high - students miss this constantly. The loop: carotid sinus -> CN IX to the solitary nucleus -> CN X to nodal tissue. BP, CN IX, and CN X all move the SAME direction; sympathetic activity moves opposite. Low BP -> low IX -> low X -> heart rate up." },
  { id: "ar159", category: "Cardio", q: "65M with orthopnea and paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea - arrows for pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure, alveolar interstitial hydrostatic pressure, alveolar interstitial oncotic pressure?", a: "↑capillary hydrostatic, ↑alveolar interstitial hydrostatic, ↓alveolar interstitial oncotic. Left heart failure backs pressure into the lungs: high left atrial pressure = high PCWP = high capillary hydrostatic pressure, which pushes fluid into the alveolar spaces. Hydrostatic pressure is fluid pressure and pushes OUT; oncotic pressure is solute pressure and holds fluid IN. The fluid that leaks is TRANSUDATE - low in protein, cells, and LDH - which dilutes the interstitial oncotic pressure." },
  { id: "ar160", category: "Pulm", q: "28F non-smoker with 6 months of dyspnea, increased pulmonary vascular markings, dilated pulmonary artery, loud P2 - arrows for PCWP, endothelin-1, pulmonary vascular resistance, systemic vascular resistance?", a: "↔PCWP, ↑endothelin-1, ↑pulmonary vascular resistance, ↔systemic vascular resistance. Primary pulmonary hypertension, usually BMPR2 mutations, classically a non-smoking woman in her 20s-30s. PCWP is normal because the left heart is fine - this question exists to test whether you know PCWP is only high in LEFT heart problems. Endothelin-1 (a potent vasoconstrictor) is up and nitric oxide synthase down; bosentan is the endothelin receptor antagonist." },
  { id: "ar161", category: "Pleural Fluid", q: "55M with long-standing hypertension, pleural effusion, diffuse infiltrates, and LBBB - arrows for pleural fluid LDH, protein, glucose?", a: "↓LDH, ↓protein, ↑glucose. This is a TRANSUDATE from high pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure (LBBB means LVH). Pleural:serum LDH <0.6 and protein <0.5 = transudate; above those = exudate. Low pleural glucose means bacterial or fungal infection consuming it, so with no infection suspected, glucose stays near the serum value - a 20 would be empyema, an 80 is transudate." },
  { id: "ar162", category: "Cardio", q: "45F with type II diabetes started on enalapril - arrows for systemic vascular resistance, renal efferent arteriolar diameter, heart rate, ejection fraction?", a: "↓SVR, ↑efferent diameter, ↑heart rate, ↑ejection fraction. Blocking AT II relieves peripheral and efferent constriction; the drop in BP triggers a reflex rise in heart rate; and less afterload means a higher ejection fraction, which is why ACEi/ARBs are first-line in heart failure." },
  { id: "ar163", category: "Cardio", q: "Which drugs dilate arterioles (and raise heart rate) vs constrict them?", a: "Dilate -> BP down -> reflex heart rate UP: ACE inhibitors, ARBs, dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (nifedipine), alpha-1 antagonists (prazosin), hydralazine. Constrict -> BP up -> reflex heart rate DOWN: alpha-1 agonists (phenylephrine, oxymetazoline, midodrine)." },
  { id: "ar164", category: "Cardio", q: "15M jumps into a cold lake - arrows for central blood volume, ANP secretion, ADH release?", a: "↑central blood volume, ↑ANP, ↓ADH. Cold causes peripheral vasoconstriction to conserve heat, shifting blood centrally; atrial myocytes secrete ANP in response to stretch. ANP is the body's natural diuretic - it inhibits renin release and reduces renal sodium retention." },
  { id: "ar165", category: "Cardio", q: "What is aldosterone escape?", a: "High aldosterone raises blood volume, which stretches the atria and raises ANP, and ANP blunts aldosterone's effect at the kidney by preventing sodium reabsorption. It is the negative feedback that keeps runaway aldosterone in check." },
  { id: "ar166", category: "Congenital Heart", q: "Neonate whose VSD is repaired with a prosthetic patch - arrows for LV pressure, RV pressure, left atrial pressure?", a: "↑LV pressure, ↓RV pressure, ↓left atrial pressure. With the shunt closed, less blood leaves the LV for the RV, so LV pressure rises and RV pressure falls; less blood through the right heart means less returning through the lungs, so left atrial preload and pressure fall. This exact combination is repeated on offline and online Step 1 NBMEs." },
  { id: "ar167", category: "Congenital Heart", q: "15F with fixed splitting of S2 - arrows for SVC pO2, right atrial pO2, right ventricular pO2, pulmonary artery pO2?", a: "↔SVC, ↑RA, ↑RV, ↑pulmonary artery. Atrial septal defect: left-sided pressures are higher, so oxygenated blood crosses into the right atrium and carries that step-up downstream. The vena cavae are proximal to the defect, so they are unchanged. The USMLE usually runs this backwards - giving chamber oxygen values and asking you to name the defect." },
  { id: "ar168", category: "Congenital Heart", q: "4M with a holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border, VSD and left atrial dilatation on echo - arrows for SVC, RA, RV, LV, and pulmonary artery pO2?", a: "↔SVC, ↔RA, ↑RV, ↔LV, ↑pulmonary artery. VSD: the step-up starts at the ventricle, so everything proximal (SVC, RA) is normal. The left-to-right shunt raises RV preload and sends more blood through the lungs, dilating the left atrium. LV pO2 is normal because flow only goes left to right, high pressure to low." },
  { id: "ar169", category: "Cardio", q: "24M standing up from supine - arrows for venous return, carotid sinus baroreceptor activity, cerebral blood flow?", a: "ALL ↓. Standing pools blood in the veins, so venous return, central blood volume, and cardiac output fall. Baroreceptor firing is stretch-dependent, so lower pressure means LOWER firing, and cerebral blood flow drops with the cardiac output." },
  { id: "ar170", category: "Cardio", q: "50M started on propranolol - arrows for LV end-diastolic volume, cardiac output, systemic vascular resistance?", a: "↑LVEDV, ↓cardiac output, ↑SVR. Beta-1 blockade lowers rate and contractility; a slower rate means more time in diastole, so the ventricle fills more and end-diastolic volume rises. Net myocardial oxygen demand still falls, because lower chronotropy and inotropy outweigh the extra preload. Beta-2 blockade removes peripheral vasodilation, so systemic resistance rises." },
  { id: "ar171", category: "Cardio", q: "55M started on nitrates for ischemic heart disease - arrows for venous return, heart rate, cardiac output?", a: "↓venous return, ↑heart rate, ↓cardiac output. Nitrates VENOdilate, pooling blood and cutting preload; the lower output means less carotid sinus stretch, less CN IX, less vagal CN X, so heart rate rises. Even with a faster rate, the drop in preload wins and oxygen demand falls." },
  { id: "ar172", category: "Cardio", q: "42F started on nifedipine - arrows for systemic vascular resistance, heart rate, LV ejection fraction?", a: "↓SVR, ↑heart rate, ↑ejection fraction. Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers dilate ARTERIOLES (nitrates dilate veins). Lower aortic pressure means less baroreceptor stretch and a reflex tachycardia; less afterload means a higher ejection fraction. ACE inhibitors, ARBs, alpha-1 blockers, and hydralazine give the same arrows." },
  { id: "ar173", category: "Heart Failure", q: "67M with exertional dyspnea, a dilated cardiac silhouette, and an S3 - arrows for LV ejection fraction, LV end-diastolic pressure, LV end-diastolic volume?", a: "↓ejection fraction, ↑end-diastolic pressure, ↑end-diastolic volume. Dilated cardiomyopathy with SYSTOLIC dysfunction. S3 = dilated LV = volume (preload) overload = the heart cannot contract properly. S3 can be physiologic in endurance athletes and pregnancy." },
  { id: "ar174", category: "Heart Failure", q: "65F with hypertension, JVD, peripheral edema, and an S4 - arrows for LV ejection fraction, LV end-diastolic pressure, LV end-diastolic volume?", a: "↔ejection fraction, ↑end-diastolic pressure, ↔end-diastolic volume. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with DIASTOLIC dysfunction. S4 = stiff LV = pressure (afterload) overload = the heart cannot expand properly, though it still reaches normal volume - it just needs more pressure to get there. The highest-yield point is that ejection fraction is NOT reduced." },
  { id: "ar175", category: "Heart Failure", q: "Systolic vs diastolic dysfunction summary?", a: "S3 = dilated LV = volume/preload overload = systolic dysfunction = dilated cardiomyopathy: ↓EF, ↑EDP, ↑EDV. S4 = stiff LV = pressure/afterload overload = diastolic dysfunction = restrictive and hypertrophic cardiomyopathies: ↔EF, ↑EDP, ↔EDV. Note S4 is not always left-sided - 2CK gives right-sided S4s from pulmonary hypertension or severe mitral stenosis." },
  { id: "ar176", category: "Shock", q: "67M with crushing chest pain, anterior ST elevations, BP 85/50 - arrows for CO2, pH, HCO3-, SVR, PCWP, cardiac output?", a: "↓CO2, ↓pH, ↓HCO3-, ↑SVR, ↑PCWP, ↓cardiac output. Cardiogenic shock: any shock causes lactic acidosis from peripheral anaerobic glycolysis, so bicarb falls and CO2 is blown off. SVR rises to compensate for the failing pump, and PCWP is high because the left heart is involved - isolated right heart MIs are rare. Lactic acidosis in shock is exceedingly high-yield." },
  { id: "ar177", category: "Shock", q: "15F stung by a bee, BP 80/40, RR 30, HR 120 - arrows for cardiac output, SVR, PCWP?", a: "↑cardiac output, ↓SVR, ↓PCWP. Anaphylactic shock, a type I hypersensitivity: IgE crosslinking on mast cells and basophils releases histamine and prostaglandin (which recruit eosinophils), and those vasodilate, dropping SVR. Cardiac output is HIGH because venous return is still high and the heart is fast - the textbooks say output falls in anaphylaxis, but that is wrong on the USMLE." },
  { id: "ar178", category: "Shock", q: "How does PCWP sort out shock?", a: "Look at PCWP first. HIGH = cardiogenic shock (or left heart pathology) - the pump has failed. Not high, whether low or normal, rules cardiogenic shock out, and you use the other variables to sort the rest. Per Mehlman, misunderstanding PCWP is the single most common reason students miss shock questions." },
  { id: "ar179", category: "Shock", q: "34F with a worsening skin infection, fever 103, BP 80/40, HR 120 - arrows for cardiac output, SVR, PCWP?", a: "↑cardiac output, ↓SVR, ↓PCWP - the same arrows as anaphylactic shock. Septic shock: TNF-alpha causes peripheral vasodilation and IL-1 causes the fever. Output is high because venous return is high; PCWP is low or normal, just not high. Do not overthink early vs late sepsis - the USMLE wants these arrows." },
  { id: "ar180", category: "Shock", q: "22M bleeding from a gunshot wound and drowsy - arrows for cardiac output, SVR, PCWP?", a: "↓cardiac output, ↑SVR, ↓PCWP. Hypovolemic shock: less volume means less output, so baroreceptor firing falls, vagal tone falls, and sympathetic firing rises - norepinephrine and epinephrine hit alpha-1 receptors on arterioles and raise resistance. PCWP is low (an NBME has it normal); the point is that it is not high." },
  { id: "ar181", category: "Shock", q: "9M after a viral infection with low BP, HR 120, an enlarged heart with hazy lung fields, and red urine - arrows for cardiac output, SVR, PCWP?", a: "↓cardiac output, ↑SVR, ↑PCWP. Cardiogenic shock from Coxsackie B dilated cardiomyopathy; the red urine is unrelated IgA nephropathy 1-2 days after a viral illness. Increased PCWP is the hallmark of cardiogenic shock, along with JVD and high central venous pressure. Heart rate can be low, normal, or high - rate tells you nothing about pump function." },
  { id: "ar182", category: "Heme", q: "12M with nosebleeds a week after a viral infection - arrows for bleeding time, PT, aPTT?", a: "↑bleeding time, ↔PT, ↔aPTT. ITP: viral infection triggers autoantibodies against platelet glycoprotein IIb/IIIa, blocking platelet AGGREGATION (do not confuse with Gp Ib, which mediates adhesion to endothelium). Bleeding time measures platelet function; PT and aPTT measure clotting factors, which are untouched here." },
  { id: "ar183", category: "Heme", q: "ITP presentation, the diagnostic pick, and treatment order?", a: "Either a school-age kid after a viral infection or a woman in her 30s-40s with random bruising, a high bleeding time, and a low platelet count. 2CK mixes it with domestic abuse: random bruising with HIGH bleeding time = ITP; random bruising with NORMAL bleeding time = domestic abuse. If forced to choose a diagnostic finding, pick the low platelet count. Treat with steroids, then IVIG, then splenectomy, in that order." },
  { id: "ar184", category: "Heme", q: "10M with blood in his knee joint after soccer whose maternal uncle died after mild head trauma - arrows for bleeding time, PT, aPTT?", a: "↔bleeding time, ↔PT, ↑aPTT. Hemophilia A (factor VIII) or B (factor IX), both X-linked recessive, A more common. Hemarthrosis in a school-age boy is the textbook vignette. Only aPTT rises because VIII and IX are intrinsic-pathway only. DDAVP treats hemophilia A only. Gradual non-response to factor replacement means antibodies against the replaced factor." },
  { id: "ar185", category: "Heme", q: "17F with nosebleeds and heavy menses with clots, normal aPTT, dad also has nosebleeds - arrows for bleeding time, PT, platelet count?", a: "↑bleeding time, ↔PT, ↔platelet count. Von Willebrand disease, autosomal dominant. vWF bridges platelet Gp Ib to endothelial collagen, so platelet ADHESION fails - vignettes mix a factor-type problem (heavy menses, bleeding after tooth extraction) with a platelet-type problem (petechiae, epistaxis). vWF also stabilizes factor VIII, so aPTT is elevated in about half of NBME vWD questions and normal in the other half - a normal aPTT does NOT rule it out. Treat with DDAVP." },
  { id: "ar186", category: "Heme", q: "29F treated for leukemia with t(15;17) now oozing from IV and catheter sites - arrows for bleeding time, PT, aPTT?", a: "ALL ↑. DIC from treatment of acute promyelocytic leukemia (AML M3) - released Auer rods (myeloperoxidase) precipitate it. Other causes: sepsis, trauma, amniotic fluid embolism. Platelets AND clotting factors are consumed, and schistocytes appear on smear." },
  { id: "ar187", category: "Heme", q: "When is bleeding from IV/catheter sites NOT DIC?", a: "After massive transfusion. A 2CK surgery form gives a man who received about 20 units of packed RBCs and then bled from IV sites, with DIC not listed - the answer is thrombocytopenia, because packed RBCs contain no platelets and cause dilutional thrombocytopenia." },
  { id: "ar188", category: "Heme", q: "30F 30 seconds after placental delivery with dyspnea, tachycardia, and bleeding from IV sites - arrows for platelet count, fibrinogen, plasmin activity, D-dimer?", a: "↓platelets, ↓fibrinogen, ↑plasmin activity, ↑D-dimer. DIC from amniotic fluid embolism. Fibrinogen is consumed making fibrin; plasmin breaks that fibrin down, and D-dimer is just fibrin degradation products. Full DIC set: increased bleeding time, PT, aPTT, plasmin, and D-dimer, with decreased platelets and fibrinogen." },
  { id: "ar189", category: "Heme", q: "Neonate born at home bleeding from the umbilical stump - arrows for bleeding time, PT, aPTT?", a: "↔bleeding time, ↑PT, ↑aPTT. Vitamin K deficiency - the classic Step presentation, or a patient on broad-spectrum antibiotics for weeks. Vitamin K activates factors II, VII, IX, X plus proteins C and S, so both PT and aPTT rise; platelets are untouched, so bleeding time is normal." },
  { id: "ar190", category: "Heme", q: "40M with chronic renal failure, high BUN and creatinine, and 2 weeks of nosebleeds - arrows for platelet count, bleeding time, PT, aPTT?", a: "↔platelet count, ↑bleeding time, ↔PT, ↔aPTT. Uremic (acquired) platelet dysfunction - high BUN makes platelets not work, a QUALITATIVE problem, so the count is normal. Uremic patients need hemodialysis before surgery to prevent excessive bleeding." },
  { id: "ar191", category: "OB", q: "33F at 30 weeks with BP 150/95, proteinuria, and schistocytes - arrows for platelet count, hematocrit, liver enzymes?", a: "↓platelets, ↓hematocrit, ↑liver enzymes. Preeclampsia progressed to HELLP - Hemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets. On the USMLE preeclampsia is proteinuria plus hypertension after 20 weeks; add seizures and it is eclampsia. The transaminases rise from fibrin deposits in hepatic sinusoids obstructing flow." },
  { id: "ar192", category: "Anemia", q: "30F with normal periods and pale RBCs on smear - arrows for MCV, hematocrit, serum iron, ferritin, transferrin saturation, RDW?", a: "↓MCV, hematocrit, iron, ferritin, and transferrin saturation, with ↑RDW. Iron deficiency anemia, common in young women even without heavy menses. Ferritin is the storage form and the most sensitive indicator of iron status - low iron with NORMAL ferritin is anemia of chronic disease instead. Transferrin levels rise while its binding sites sit empty, so saturation is low." },
  { id: "ar193", category: "Anemia", q: "What is RDW and how does it separate iron deficiency from thalassemia?", a: "RDW is the range of red cell sizes. HIGH in iron deficiency, because low marrow iron makes some cells microcytic and others normocytic. LOW or low-normal in thalassemia, because defective chain production makes uniformly small cells. Per Mehlman, RDW is one of the most underrated parameters in heme questions." },
  { id: "ar194", category: "Anemia", q: "8F with recurrent joint pains, high ESR, and a salmon-pink rash - arrows for MCV, hematocrit, serum iron, ferritin, transferrin?", a: "↔MCV, ↓hematocrit, ↓iron, ↔ferritin, ↓transferrin. Juvenile rheumatoid arthritis with anemia of chronic disease. Ferritin is normal because the patient is not iron deficient - inflammation just blocks iron incorporation into heme. Transferrin is suppressed by inflammation, and transferrin SATURATION can be low-normal, normal, or high, which is why it is not a reliable arrow. Note MCV can absolutely be LOW in AoCD on 2CK peds forms - do not exclude the diagnosis over it." },
  { id: "ar195", category: "Anemia", q: "When do you treat anemia of chronic disease with EPO?", a: "Only when chronic renal failure is the cause. For any other etiology - autoimmune disease, chronic hepatitis B or C, other chronic infection - treat the underlying cause, not EPO." },
  { id: "ar196", category: "Anemia", q: "22F at 8 weeks with alpha-thalassemia on electrophoresis - arrows for MCV, hematocrit, serum iron, ferritin, RDW?", a: "↓MCV, ↓hematocrit, ↔iron, ↔ferritin, ↔RDW. Thalassemia (alpha or beta) is the answer when there is microcytic anemia with NORMAL iron studies. 2CK obgyn loves this: microcytic anemia that does not improve after three weeks of iron with normal iron and ferritin - next step is hemoglobin electrophoresis. RDW can be normal or low; the point is it is not high, because high RDW means iron deficiency." },
  { id: "ar197", category: "Anemia", q: "30F who feels restless in bed and must move her legs before sleep - arrows for MCV, hematocrit, CNS dopamine transmission?", a: "ALL ↓. Restless leg syndrome - the most common cause is iron deficiency anemia, so check iron and ferritin; if iron is normal, the mechanism is impaired dopamine transmission. Treat with iron if IDA, otherwise a D2 agonist like pramipexole or ropinirole. These patients have increased risk of Parkinson disease, which fits, since Parkinson is loss of dopaminergic neurons." },
  { id: "ar198", category: "Anemia", q: "22F with iron deficiency anemia compared to a normal person - arrows for arterial pO2, hemoglobin saturation, arterial O2 content?", a: "↔arterial pO2, ↔hemoglobin saturation, ↓arterial O2 content. NBME repeats this one. Arterial O2 content = dissolved O2 (pO2) + O2 bound to hemoglobin. The lungs work fine, so pO2 is normal and the available hemoglobin saturates normally - there is just less hemoglobin, so total oxygen carried is down. Saturation cannot rise above normal because hemoglobin is already maximally saturated in healthy people." },
  { id: "ar199", category: "Bilirubin", q: "21F stressed for exams with 2 days of yellow eyes and prior similar episodes - arrows for hematocrit, indirect bilirubin, direct bilirubin, total bilirubin?", a: "↔hematocrit, ↑indirect bilirubin, ↔direct bilirubin, ↑total bilirubin. Gilbert syndrome - decreased hepatic uptake and UDP-glucuronosyltransferase activity, benign, no treatment needed." },
  { id: "ar200", category: "Bilirubin", q: "Indirect vs direct bilirubin - what raises each?", a: "Indirect (unconjugated) is not water-soluble and exists after RBC breakdown but before the liver: high in hemolysis (hemolytic anemia, spherocytosis, G6PD deficiency), sickle cell, and Gilbert. Direct (conjugated) is water-soluble and exists after hepatic conjugation into bile: high in post-hepatic obstruction (choledocholithiasis, head-of-pancreas cancer, biliary atresia) and autoimmune biliary disease (primary sclerosing cholangitis, primary biliary cirrhosis)." },
  { id: "ar201", category: "Bilirubin", q: "57F jaundiced with pale stools and dark urine one week after cholecystectomy - arrows for hematocrit, indirect bilirubin, direct bilirubin, total bilirubin?", a: "↔hematocrit, ↔indirect, ↑direct, ↑total. Choledocholithiasis - a stone in the biliary tree (not cholelithiasis, which is in the gallbladder); a retained stone can descend into the common bile duct after surgery. Post-hepatic obstruction raises DIRECT bilirubin, less pigment reaches the gut so stools are acholic, and water-soluble direct bilirubin spills into the urine, darkening it." },
  { id: "ar202", category: "Bilirubin", q: "10M who recently took dapsone, now with yellow eyes - arrows for hematocrit, indirect bilirubin, direct bilirubin, total bilirubin?", a: "↓hematocrit, ↑indirect, ↔direct, ↑total. G6PD deficiency - hemolysis triggered by dapsone, primaquine, or fava beans. Less NADPH means RBCs cannot handle oxidative stress. In hemolysis the rise is predominantly INDIRECT, since the liver easily handles the extra load; direct may be normal or barely up." },
  { id: "ar203", category: "Metabolism", q: "20M eats a large turkey dinner - arrows for glycogen phosphorylase, glucokinase, PEP carboxykinase, pyruvate carboxylase?", a: "↓glycogen phosphorylase, ↑glucokinase, ↓PEP carboxykinase, ↓pyruvate carboxylase. Ask fed or fasting first - he is FED. Glycogen phosphorylase breaks glycogen down, so it is off; glucokinase is the liver hexokinase and the first step of glycolysis, so it is on; PEP carboxykinase and pyruvate carboxylase are gluconeogenic, so both are off." },
  { id: "ar204", category: "Metabolism", q: "20M eats a turkey dinner - arrows for glycogen synthase, PFK-2, glucose-6-phosphatase, pyruvate dehydrogenase?", a: "↑glycogen synthase, ↑PFK-2, ↓glucose-6-phosphatase, ↑pyruvate dehydrogenase. Fed state: glycogen is built, PFK-2 makes F-2,6-BP which allosterically drives PFK-1 and glycolysis forward, glucose-6-phosphatase (gluconeogenic, deficient in von Gierke) is off, and pyruvate dehydrogenase sends pyruvate to acetyl-CoA. Do not confuse pyruvate DEHYDROGENASE with pyruvate CARBOXYLASE, which is gluconeogenic." },
  { id: "ar205", category: "Metabolism", q: "10F with a viral infection, polyuria, polydipsia - arrows for GLUT-4 expression, lipolysis, glucokinase, serine-threonine kinase activity, ubiquitin-mediated proteolysis?", a: "↓GLUT-4, ↑lipolysis, ↓glucokinase, ↓serine-threonine kinase activity, ↑ubiquitin-mediated proteolysis. DKA with no insulin: GLUT-4 and hepatic glucokinase are insulin-driven, so both fall; lipolysis, glycogenolysis, and gluconeogenesis all rise for energy; insulin normally raises MAP tyrosine kinase and serine-threonine kinase activity; and without anabolic insulin, proteins are tagged with ubiquitin and broken down for amino acids." },
  { id: "ar206", category: "Enzyme Kinetics", q: "Glucokinase vs hexokinase - arrows for Km and Vmax?", a: "Glucokinase has ↑Km and ↑Vmax compared to hexokinase - lower affinity, higher capacity. High Km = low affinity; high Vmax = high capacity. Hexokinase is everywhere; glucokinase is mostly liver. The liver should not grab glucose when blood levels are low (other cells need it), but when glucose and insulin are high, glucokinase turns on and buffers the load into glycogen - which is why it also needs the higher capacity." },
  { id: "ar207", category: "Cell Signaling", q: "25F at 40 weeks given oxytocin - arrows for IP3, DAG, cAMP?", a: "↑IP3, ↑DAG, ↔cAMP. Oxytocin works through G-alpha-q. The NBME asks about oxytocin receptor binding across multiple forms." },
  { id: "ar208", category: "Cell Signaling", q: "43M treated with cimetidine for gastric ulcers - arrows for IP3, DAG, cAMP?", a: "↔IP3, ↔DAG, ↓cAMP. Cimetidine is an H2 blocker, and the H2 receptor is G-alpha-s, so blocking it lowers cAMP. Cimetidine also causes gynecomastia." },
  { id: "ar209", category: "Electrolytes", q: "24M lost in the woods for 3 weeks, BMI 27, given a big meal - arrows for serum K+ and PO4?", a: "BOTH ↓. Refeeding syndrome. Hypophosphatemia is the main disturbance they test: rapid synthesis of glycolytic intermediates traps phosphate. Hypokalemia follows the insulin spike driving potassium into cells. BMI can be low, normal, or HIGH - do not be thrown by a normal BMI. Monitor electrolytes closely to prevent arrhythmia." },
];

export default arrowsQuestions;
